const protoCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Fidelity is a budget decision', note: 'Sketches and wireframes (minutes — explore broadly), clickable prototypes (hours — test flows), high-fidelity (days — test details): match fidelity to the question, because polish INVITES critique of polish when you wanted critique of the idea.' },
    { math: '2 · Wireframes are arguments', note: 'Grey boxes and lines: hierarchy, sequence, priority — everything a layout must decide before decoration joins the meeting. The wireframe is where the grids lesson\'s structure and the research lesson\'s flows meet.' },
    { math: '3 · Prototype the risk, not the product', note: 'Build the smallest testable version of the riskiest assumption (the entrepreneurship lesson\'s MVP logic): a paper prototype of the checkout, a landing page for demand. Speed is the point — wrong cheaply beats wrong expensively.' },
    { math: '4 · Tools, in sequence', note: 'Paper → Figma/ Sketch (design-and-prototype tools) → code when the design stabilises; hand-off specs (tokens, components) keep developers building instead of guessing. The prototype is disposable; the learning is not.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/prototyping-and-wireframes">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Prototyping is <strong>making ideas testable before making them expensive</strong> — the
    engineering lesson\'s estimation discipline meets the research lesson\'s users. Fidelity is
    a variable you set deliberately, per question.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="fidelity as budget, wireframes as arguments, risk-first, tools" widget="equationStep" config={protoCfg} />

  <h2>The testing loop in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wizard of Oz:</strong> fake the back end with a human behind the curtain — the AI lesson\'s "mechanical turk" approach: test whether users want the feature before building whether it can exist.</li>
    <li><strong>Prototype the unhappy path:</strong> demos show the sunny flow; users live in the errors, empty states and slow networks. Loading, failure, zero-data states are half the real design (the error-message principle of the design lesson).</li>
    <li><strong>Remote testing matured:</strong> async video tasks and unmoderated tests buy volume and speed — with the caveat that you lose the observer\'s nuance; pair remote volume with moderated depth (the research lesson\'s triangulation).</li>
    <li><strong>Hand-off is a design deliverable:</strong> the systems lesson\'s component logic — prototypes built from real components hand off cleanly; the throwaway-beauty-prototype is where design debt is born (the design-systems lesson\'s argument).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Buxton, Sketching User Experiences; the entrepreneurship lesson\'s MVP logic; the research lesson\'s usability loop.</p>
</TopicPage>
